scenario_questions.scenario:
A nightly ETL on EC2 reads from s3://data-bucket/raw/ and writes to s3://data-bucket/processed/. You enabled the following lifecycle on the bucket:

```json
{
  "Rules": [
    {
      "ID": "aggressive-archive",
      "Status": "Enabled",
      "Filter": {"Prefix": "raw/"},
      "Transitions": [ {"Days": 1, "StorageClass": "GLACIER"} ],
      "NoncurrentVersionTransitions": [ {"NoncurrentDays": 1, "StorageClass": "GLACIER"} ]
    },
    {
      "ID": "processed-ia-30",
      "Status": "Enabled",
      "Filter": {"Prefix": "processed/"},
      "Transitions": [ {"Days": 30, "StorageClass": "STANDARD_IA"} ]
    }
  ]
}
```
After enabling, the next ETL run became extremely slow and expensive. You cannot change the ETL to restore GLACIER objects before reading. Storage cost should still be optimized over time.

concept_mcqs.stem:
Users report intermittent 503s after moving to HTTPS-only. Target group health checks show flapping. Current config and server behavior:

```json
{
  "TargetGroup": {
    "Protocol": "HTTP",
    "Port": 80,
    "HealthCheckProtocol": "HTTP",
    "HealthCheckPort": "80",
    "HealthCheckPath": "/health",
    "Matcher": {"HttpCode": "200"}
  }
}
```

```nginx
server {
  listen 80;
  return 301 https://$host$request_uri;
}
```

Which change will stop the flapping and validate the true health path?

scenario_questions.scenario:
You operate a stateless web app behind an ALB with an Auto Scaling group across two AZs. Deployments replace instances gradually. During deployments, synthetic monitoring shows brief spikes of 502/504 and user session drops immediately after new instances register healthy. Current settings:

```json
{
  "TargetGroup": {
    "HealthCheckPath": "/health",
    "HealthCheckProtocol": "HTTPS",
    "Matcher": {"HttpCode": "200"},
    "slow_start.duration_seconds": 0,
    "deregistration_delay.timeout_seconds": 300
  },
  "ALB": {"cross_zone.enabled": true},
  "ASG": {
    "HealthCheckType": "ELB",
    "HealthCheckGracePeriod": 60,
    "DefaultInstanceWarmup": 0
  }
}
```

The app needs ~90 seconds to warm caches after boot but returns 200 on `/health` almost immediately. You cannot change app code this sprint.

concept_mcqs.stem:
You connected a Lambda function to API Gateway (Lambda proxy integration). The handler is:
```javascript
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  return { message: "ok" };
};
```
A client performs:
```http
GET /prod/ping HTTP/1.1
Host: api.example.com
```
What does the client receive and why?

Your Lambda behind API Gateway sometimes takes ~30 seconds. The handler simulates work:
```javascript
exports.handler = async () => {
  await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 30000));
  return { statusCode: 200, body: "done" };
};
```
Clients receive a 504 after ~29 seconds, but CloudWatch Logs show the function completes at ~30 seconds. What happened?
